{{trail|Astronomy}}[[File:SR-weather-Sun.jpg|thumb|left|The sun]]<onlyinclude>
The [[Lore:Sun|sun]] (also known as '''Magnus''',{{ref|[[General:Spread Holiday Cheer During The New Life Festival|Spread Holiday Cheer During The New Life Festival]]}} '''Daibethe''' in [[Lore:Yoku|Yoku]],{{Ref|name=DFHolidays|[[Daggerfall:Holidays#Drigh R'Zimb|Drigh R'Zimb]] holiday description in ''[[Daggerfall:Daggerfall|Daggerfall]]''}} '''Shul''' in the [[Lore:Dragon Language|Dragon language]],{{Ref|name=SRBreath|[[Skyrim:Fire Breath|Fire Breath]] in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} and '''Vakka''' in [[Lore:Jel|Jel]]){{Ref|name=TST:AJP|{{Cite Book|The Sharper Tongue: A Jel Primer}}}} is the common term for the largest tear in reality between the [[Lore:Mundus|Mundus]] and [[Lore:Aetherius|Aetherius]]. It appears as a bright orb in the skies of [[Lore:Nirn|Nirn]] and is the main source of light and [[Lore:Magic|magicka]] in the world, providing a constant flow from Aetherius.{{ref|name=Arena Supermundus|{{Cite Book|Pocket Guide to the Empire, 3rd Edition/Arena Supermundus}}}}</onlyinclude>

It is widely considered synonymous with as well as a creation of the Aedra '''[[Lore:Magnus|Magnus]]'''. The common story for its creation is that it formed when Magnus fled the mortal world in the final stages of its creation, to prevent himself being trapped.{{Ref|name=TM|{{Cite Book|The Monomyth}}}} He was joined by the [[Lore:Magna Ge|Magna Ge]], who left similar but smaller "holes" which became known as the [[Lore:Stars|stars]].{{ref|name=EoMN|{{Cite Book|Exegesis of Merid-Nunda}}}}{{Ref|name=TM}}{{Ref|name=VoFitE|{{Cite Book|Varieties of Faith in the Empire}}}}{{Ref|name=36LoV33|{{Cite Book|36 Lessons of Vivec, Sermon 33}}}} In the Anuadic creation story, prior to the shattering of the twelve worlds and the formation of Nirn, [[Lore:Anu|Anu]] in his grief at the loss of [[Lore:Nir|Nir]], hid himself in the sun and slept.{{Ref|name=TAA|{{Cite Book|The Annotated Anuad}}}}  

Sunlight is necessary for surface plantlife to grow.{{Ref|name=NNSS|{{Cite Book|Naril's Note: Simulated Sunlight}}}} In locations which lack natural sunlight, such as the [[Lore:Clockwork City|Clockwork City]], biological plantlife will not thrive unless the lifegiving properties of Nirn's sun can be simulated successfully.{{Ref|name=NNSOOT|{{Cite Book|Naril's Note: Success Out of Tragedy}}}} This was discovered by the Clockwork Apostles from the Everwound Wellspring, and to produce viable amounts for a harvest, the life force of beings would be required to simulate on a small scale what the Sun produces naturally.{{Ref|name=NNSOOT}}{{Ref|name=ESOOIAMD|Events from [[ON:Oasis in a Metal Desert|Oasis in a Metal Desert]] in {{g|clockwork city|eso}}}} 

However, despite the lifegiving properties of the sun, health issues can occur due to overexposure. This is colloquially known as sunburn or "Aetherial exposure", various alchemical concoctions can be used to lessen the effects.{{Ref|name=JOSC|[[Online:Contraband J#Jar of Sun Cream|Jar of Sun Cream]] item description in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} For [[Lore:Vampire|vampire]]s, Aetherial exposure has more extreme effects, as most of the vampire breeds will burn when they enter direct sunlight.{{Ref|name=OOM|[[Online:Orb of Magnus|Orb of Magnus]] item description from [[ON:Online|ESO]]}}

The 1st day of [[Lore:Midyear|Midyear]] is considered the hottest day of the year.{{Ref|name=DFHolidays}} The light of the sun can sometimes be blocked from reaching Nirn by the movements of the [[Lore:Moons|moons]] in an event known as an '''[[Lore:Eclipse|eclipse]]''', also known as a [[Lore:Vampire|Vampire]] Day{{ref|name=ESOTwitter|group=UOL|[[General:Twitter Archive|Elder Scrolls Online Official Twitter account on Solar Eclipses]]}}.{{ref|name=CTEclipse|[[Castles:Rulings#Eclipse|Eclipse rulings]] in ''[[Castles:Castles|Castles]]''}} Sunlight may also be blotted out by magical means.{{ref|Effects of [[Skyrim:Bloodcursed Elven Arrow|Bloodcursed Elven Arrow]]s in the [[Skyrim:Dawnguard|Dawnguard]] DLC for ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Such events are considered dangerous, as the darkness makes it difficult to work without artificial light, and the lack of sunlight provides cover for vampires, [[Lore:Ghost|ghost]]s, and other [[Lore:Undead|undead]] to run amok, leading to increased activity from such beings.{{ref|name=CTEclipse}}{{ref|[[Skyrim:Lord Harkon|Lord Harkon]]'s dialogue in the [[Skyrim:Dawnguard|Dawnguard]] DLC for ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} The phenomenon is sometimes thought to represent the loss of the favor of [[Lore:Akatosh|Akatosh]] and to be possible to undo with diligent prayer to that deity. Tamrielic kings have also been known to sometimes use [[Lore:Time Magic|time altering]] magical items such as Arcane Hourglasses to fast forward time and undo the phenomenon. Inhabitants of Tamriel can sometimes receive [[Lore:Sight|warning]] of an impending eclipse in the form of prophetic nightmares revolving around it and the subsequent surge of spirits and undead.{{ref|name=CTEclipse|[[Castles:Rulings#Eclipse|Eclipse rulings]] in ''[[Castles:Castles|Castles]]''}}{{Ref|name=CastlesItems|[[Castles:Items|Arcane Hourglass]] item and use in ''[[Castles:Castles|Castles]]''}}
{{NewLine}}

==Cultural Interpretations==
===Altmer===
[[File:ON-furnishing-Prismatic Sunbird Feather 02.png|thumb|right|Prismatic Sunbird Feather]]
The Altmer consider sunlight to be the radiance of Magnus.{{Ref|name=SalasEnAD|[[Online:Alliance Soldier Dialogue|Dominion soldiers dialogue]] in [[ON:Salas En|Salas En]]}} The [[Lore:Sun Birds of Alinor|Sun Birds of Alinor]] were [[Lore:Altmer|Altmer]] of the Merethic Era who were known to have traveled to Aetherius in ships called sunbirds.{{ref|name=PGE3|{{Cite Book|Pocket Guide to the Empire, 3rd Edition/Arena Supermundus}}}} Sunbirds have been described as being made of crystal and solidified sunlight; having gem-encrusted mini-trebuchets, fit for sailing, that fire pure aetheric fire; and having banners listing their ancestors all the way back to the Dawn.{{ref|name=MKP|group=UOL|[[General:Michael Kirkbride's Posts|Michael Kirkbride's Posts]]}}

One of the [[Lore:Summerset Isles|Summerset Isles]]' major cities is [[Lore:Sunhold|Sunhold]] on the south coast of the [[Lore:Summerset Isle|main island]].{{Ref|name=LOMap|[[:File:LO-map-Summerset Isle (Anthology).jpg|Map of the Summerset Isles – ''The Elder Scrolls Anthology'']]}} The city's symbol is a yellow sun with a red outline on a white background.{{Ref|name=ONBanner|[[:File:ON-banner-Sunhold.png|Banner of Sunhold – The Elder Scrolls Online]]}} The way the sun sets over the southeastern mountains and covers the coast in shadows is how the city of [[Lore:Dusk|Dusk]] acquired its name.{{Ref|name=ESOLiveSF|''[[General:ESO Summerfall Event Special Livestream#Dusk Keep|ESO Summerfall Event Special Livestream]]''}}

Three is one of the Sacred Numbers, which the Altmer recognize as integral to the universe's existence. Three, in particular, represents the Prime [[Lore:Celestial|Celestials]], which are embodied by the Sun and the two moons.{{Ref|name=TOTSN|{{Cite Book|Thoughts on the Sacred Numbers}}}}
{{NewLine}}
===Argonian===
[[File:ON-item-Vakka Stone.jpg|Vakka Stone|thumb|right]]
Marsh-born Argonians enjoy basking in the sun.{{ref|[[Online:Runs-in-Wild|Runs-in-Wild]]'s dialogue after helping Captain Rana at the docks during [[Online:Breaking the Tide|Breaking the Tide]] in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} Contrary to what some foreign scholars believe, Argonians do not worship the sun. Rather, they enjoy its warmth{{ref|[[Online:Plays-In-Puddles|Plays-In-Puddles]]' dialogue in [[ON:Online|ESO]]: ''[[ON:Dark Brotherhood (DLC)|Dark Brotherhood]]''}} and bask in its light.{{ref|name=TSTAJP|{{Cite Book|The Sharper Tongue: A Jel Primer}}}} Argonian ladies may carry a parasol to keep their scales from drying out in the hot sun.{{ref|[[Online:Contraband P#Panther Skin Sun Parasol|Panther Skin Sun Parasol]] item description in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} Reclusive Argonians that live in places away from sunlight (such as subterranean caverns) will lose the color in their scales.{{ref|name=ONMarshmistPaleskin|[[Online:Marshmist Palescale|Marshmist Palescale]] skin description in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}}

[[Lore:Vakka Stone|Vakka Stone]]s (literally "Sun Stones" in the [[Lore:Jel|Jel]] language){{Ref|name=TST:AJP}} are bright yellow crystals used by the ancient [[Lore:Argonian|Argonian]]s to store the raw magicka that falls from the sun.{{ref|name=Kass|[[ON:Kassandra|Kassandra]]'s dialogue during [[ON:Whispers in the Wood|Whispers in the Wood]] in {{g|murkmire|eso}}}} 

Prior to [[Lore:Duskfall|Duskfall]], some tribes of ancient [[Lore:Argonian|Argonian]]s were directed by their [[Lore:Hist|Hist]] tree to worship the [[Lore:Magnus|sun]] or even to collect its power.{{ref|name=VBX|[[ON:Vakka-Bok Xanmeer|Vakka-Bok Xanmeer]] in [[ESO:Online|ESO]]}} Known as the Sun-Blessed, they were eventually betrayed by their [[Lore:Sithis|Sithis]]-worshipping brethren, and the practice faded into obscurity.{{ref|{{Cite Book|In Accord With Those Sun-Blessed}}}}{{ref|{{Cite Book|The Last Wish of the Sun-Blessed}}}}

Another myth associated with the pre-Duskfall Argonians, is of a tribe of golden-scaled Argonians that built their city upwards to reach the sun. However the ending of the story can differ, with one version claiming the sun destroyed them, while another says the tribe cracked the sun open "like an egg" and became gods.{{Ref|name=LTOTE2|{{Cite Book|Lost Tales of the Famed Explorer: Fragment II}}}}
{{NewLine}}

===Breton===
The Priory of the Golden Staff were peaceful followers of Magnus headquartered near [[Lore:Stormhaven|Stormhaven]],{{Ref|name=MtC:SC|{{Cite Book|Meet the Character - Sister Celdina}}}} teaching of Magnus's grand design for the Mundus, and his disappointment at the flaws introduced into his creation. According to them Magnus watches from the sun, waiting to repair what the others set amiss.{{Ref|name=DCT|{{Cite Book|ns_base=Online|Doombringer Celdina's Testament}}}}{{Ref|name=SC|[[ON:Sister Celdina|Sister Celdina]]'s dialogue in {{g|deadlands|eso}}}} [[Lore:Nynelle Dumaris|Nynelle Dumaris]], a famous Breton poet authored a poem ''[[Lore:Fearless as the Sun|Fearless as the Sun]]''.{{Ref|name=FATS|{{Cite Book|Fearless as the Sun}}}}

King [[Lore:Emeric|Emeric]] of [[Lore:Wayrest (kingdom)|Wayrest]] wrote in his autobiography, ''[[Lore:Triumphs of a Monarch|Triumphs of a Monarch]]'', that a Halo of Gold outlined the sun on the day of his coronation. This was supposedly an omen of approval from the Divines that ended doubts to his rule and turned even his most envious rivals into allies.{{Ref|name=TOAM10|{{Cite Book|Triumphs of a Monarch, Ch. 10}}}}

===Dunmer===
''"Under sun and sky. We greet you warmly"'' is a common greeting in Morrowind.{{Ref|name=MWGenGreeting|[[Morrowind:Generic Dialogue Voiced#Dark Elf Male|Dark Elf]] generic greeting in ''[[Morrowind:Morrowind|Morrowind]]''}} [[Lore:Morrowind|Morrowind]] legends, specifically from [[Lore:Vivec|Vivec]]'s famous sermons claim that [[Lore:Nerevar|Nerevar]] was banished from the library of the sun by the power of Magnus.{{ref|name=LOV|{{Cite Book|The 36 Lessons of Vivec}}}}
{{NewLine}}

===Dwemer===
[[File:ON-crown store-Dwarven Miniature Sun, Portable.jpg|thumb|right|Dwarven Miniature Sun]]
According to scholars studying Dwarven lore, the Dwemer paid tribute to the sun in form of the "Solar Arc" iconography. Some of their [[Lore:Dwemer Animunculi|animunculi]] were surmounted with double golden crescents representing the sun. It was a rare occurrence, however.{{ref|name=SADS|[[ON:Solar Arc Dwarven Spider|Solar Arc Dwarven Spider]]'s description in [[ON:Online|ESO]]}} As they often lived underground, they created large "miniature suns" to light the dark caves.{{Ref|name=MDS|[[ON:Miniature Dwarven Sun|Miniature Dwarven Sun]] memento}}
{{NewLine}}
===Imperial===
A shrine to [[Lore:Tiber Septim|Tiber Septim]] was built at [[Lore:Sancre Tor|Sancre Tor]] by the [[Lore:Blades|Blades]] to house the [[Lore:Armor of Tiber Septim|Armor of Tiber Septim]]. The shrine was once an important Imperial pilgrimage site, and a riddle referring to the Sun's course across the sky was used to test pilgrims who came to witness the armor.{{ref|[[OBMobile:Blood of the Divine|Baynham's riddle]] in ''[[OBMobile:Oblivion Mobile|Oblivion Mobile]]''}}

===Khajiit===
[[File:ON-crown store-Dragonscale Solar Horse.jpg|A Solar Horse, believed to be a creature of Magrus|thumb|right]]
The oppressive climate of Elsweyr makes it impractical to wear heavy clothing and armor. The typical [[#Morphology|bipedal Khajiit]] dresses in an ample shawl of brightly-colored cloth, known as a budi, for defense against the harsh rays of the sun. This shirt is tightly fastened in braids down the right side, to prevent any part of the torso fur from being seen, as the exposure of such in public is deemed both offensive and unsightly.{{ref|name=PGE1E|{{Cite Book|PGE|1|The Elsweyr Confederacy}}}} The Khajiit in the [[Lore:Third Era|Third Era]] would sometimes use the exclamation, ''"Desert Sun"''.{{Ref|name=DK|[[Daggerfall:Races#Khajiit|Khajiit]] in {{g|Daggerfall}}}} There is a variation of [[Lore:Rawlith Khaj|Rawlith Khaj]] based on the Sun.{{Ref|name=IWTB|{{Cite Book|Interview With Three Booksellers|ns_base=GEN}}}} 

In [[Lore:Khajiit|Khajiit]] religion [[Lore:Magrus|Magrus]] is known as the '''Sun God''' or the '''Cat's Eye'''. And like his counterpart, he is also associated with magic, and is revered by some Khajiit magi. However, there are several versions of Magrus' association with the sun.{{ref|name=TSS|{{Cite Book|The Sky Spirits}}}} In the works of [[Lore:Books by Author#Clan Mother Ahnissi|Clan Mother Ahnissi]], Magrus is given his authority over the Sun by [[Lore:Ahnurr|Ahnurr]].{{Ref|name=WoCMA|{{Cite Book|Words of Clan Mother Ahnissi}}}} However, this is elaborated on in earlier stories which predate the religious reforms of the [[Lore:Riddle'Thar Epiphany|Riddle'Thar Epiphany]] and is where his other title, the '''Third Eye of Azurah''' originates. In these tales, Magrus had been blinded in one eye while being pursued by [[Lore:Boethra|Boethra]] and [[Lore:Lorkhaj|Lorkhaj]] and then fell into the [[Lore:Oblivion|Oblivion]] realm [[Lore:Moonshadow|Moonshadow]]. Here [[Lore:Azurah|Azurah]] found and judged him, in the versions more sympathetic to Magrus, he willingly sacrifices his eye to Azurah for the benefit of the Khajiit. In the other known version, Azurah deems him as too afraid to have authority over a sphere and takes his second eye and Magrus is left blinded.{{ref|name=TSS}}

Azurah was then said to have turned the eye into a stone known as the ''Aether Prism'', which would reflect the Varliance Gate. This story is used to explain sunrise and sunset on Nirn, where it is said to be the daily opening and closing of the Aether Prism.{{ref|name=TSS}} The [[Lore:Sunspire|Sunspire]] is a holy temple dedicated to the Dragon God of Cats, [[Lore:Alkosh|Alkosh]]. It is located on the southern edge of [[Lore:Anequina|Anequina]], near the [[Lore:Tenmar Forest|Tenmar Forest]] and it is where the deity fought [[Lore:Pelinal Whitestrake|Pelinal Whitestrake]].{{Ref|name=ONSunspire|[[Online:Sunspire|Sunspire]] in [[Online:Online|ESO]]<nowiki>:</nowiki> ''[[Online:Elsweyr (DLC)|Elsweyr]]''}}{{Ref|name=ONAzin|[[Online:Moon-Bishop Azin-jo|Moon-Bishop Azin-jo]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]<nowiki>:</nowiki> ''[[Online:Elsweyr (DLC)|Elsweyr]]''}} Creatures known as [[Lore:Solar Horse|Solar Horses]] are associated with Magrus.{{ref|name=DSH|[[Online:Dragonscale Solar Horse|Dragonscale Solar Horse]] mount description in [[ON:Online|ESO]]}}
{{NewLine}}

===Nede===
The council of kings under [[Lore:Durac|High King Durac]] of Skyreach, among seven other titles, had a Sun King. These kings served as representative leaders of the various tribes under Skyreach's jurisdiction.{{ref|{{Cite Book|Saradin's Diary}}}}{{ref|[[ON:The Sun King|The Sun King]]'s appearance in [[ESO:Online|ESO]]}} The Nedes of [[Lore:Cyrodiil|Cyrodiil]] also held significance in the sun, feeling dismayed on the fifth of First Seed, {{Year|1E 393}}, when the sun darkened. They predicted a great event would occur after, which was proven correct within the year when the [[Lore:Ayleid|Ayleids]] of [[Lore:Malada|Malada]] emerged from their fane and afflicted the local farmland.{{ref|{{Cite Book|Cleansing of the Fane}}}}

===Nord===
Ancient Nords used glass-like sunstones to determine the sun's location even on a cloudy day. [[Lore:Ysgramor|Ysgramor]] was said to use one such device during his voyages.{{Ref|name=ONAntiSunstone|[[Online:Bleakrock Isle Antiquities#High King Sunstone|High King Sunstone]] antiquity codex entries in [[Online:Online|ESO]]<nowiki>:</nowiki> ''[[Online:Greymoor|Greymoor]]''}} When it was still active, the [[Lore:Meridia|Meridian]] monks of [[Lore:Kilkreath Temple|Kilkreath Temple]] in northwestern Skyrim conducted secret rituals during the feasts of High Sun and Low Sun. Travelers were advised to avoid the area during this time.{{Ref|name=GTWSH|{{Cite Book|Guide to Western Skyrim: Haafingar}}}} In [[Lore:The Pale|the Pale]], a regional type of [[Lore:Cheese|cheese]] called Bjenost is sliced into golden disks during the High Spring Fest. Locals claim it is meant to represent the sun in the springtime.{{Ref|name=COSTRTP|{{Cite Book|Cheeses of Skyrim: The Reach, The Pale}}}} [[Lore:Dawnstar|Dawnstar]]'s name is inspired by the sun as it rises over the town at dawn.{{Ref|name=ARDawnstar|[[Arena:Dawnstar|Dawnstar]] location and rumors in ''[[Arena:Arena|Arena]]''}} In southern [[Lore:Skyrim|Skyrim]], there is a stronghold on a plateau called [[Lore:Fort Sungard|Fort Sungard]] and it protects the road between [[Lore:The Reach|the Reach]] and central Skyrim.{{Ref|name=SRSungard|[[Skyrim:Fort Sungard|Fort Sungard]] in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}

===Orc===
The practice known as the death of sun and feathers was a form of execution practiced by the [[Lore:Iron Orcs|Iron Orcs]]. The sentenced was nailed to the stone and cut once for each of the ten iron oaths and then the sun burned until the crows gather. It was considered a proper death for liars and traitors.{{Ref|name=Agganor's Journal|{{Cite Book|ns_base=Online|Agganor's Journal}}}}{{ref|name=LTB|[[ON:Lashburr Tooth-Breaker|Lashburr Tooth-Breaker]]'s dialogue in [[ON:Online|ESO]]}}

===Redguard===
[[File:RG-misc-Glide Sun Compare (Glide).bmp|thumb|right|Daibethe]]
The [[Lore:Redguard|Redguards]] refer to the sun as '''Daibethe'''. On the 1st of [[Lore:Midyear|Midyear]], the people of [[Lore:Abibon-Gora|Abibon-Gora]] honor it through the festival of [[Lore:Drigh R'Zimb|Drigh R'Zimb]].{{Ref|name=DFHolidays}} The Redguard spirit of the air, [[Lore:Tava|Tava]] is often associated with elements of the sun{{Ref|name=TUAAYQ|{{Cite Book|The Unveiled Azadiyeh Answers Your Questions}}}} and factions in reverence to her such as the [[Lore:Glinting Talons|Glinting Talons]] used references to the sun and light frequently.{{Ref|name=GT|{{Cite Book|Glinting Talons (book)}}}} The Redguards in the [[Lore:Third Era|Third Era]] would sometimes use the exclamation, ''"by the Sun"''.{{Ref|name=DR|[[Daggerfall:Races#Redguard|Redguard]] in {{g|Daggerfall}}}} 

The Pillar of the Singing Sun is a cenotaph dedicated to [[Lore:Frandar Hunding|Frandar Hunding]]. It is built on a small island in [[Lore:Stros M'Kai|Stros M'Kai]], specifically west of the [[Lore:Ogres Tooth Mountains|Ogres Tooth Mountains]].{{Ref|name=AFBTW|{{Cite Book|A Fortune Behind Those Walls}}}}{{Ref|name=ONPillar|[[Online:Pillar of the Singing Sun|Pillar of the Singing Sun]] in {{G|eso}}}} The town of [[Lore:Sunkeep|Sunkeep]] is located on the western coast between [[Lore:Hegathe|Hegathe]] and [[Lore:Sentinel|Sentinel]].{{Ref|name=ARSunkeep|[[Arena:Sunkeep|Sunkeep]] in ''[[Arena:Arena|Arena]]''}}
{{NewLine}}

===Reachfolk===
According to the [[Lore:Reachfolk|Reachfolk]] legends every new year in [[Lore:The Reach|the vale]] of [[Lore:Karth River|Karth]] as autumn wanes the wolf aspect of [[Lore:Hircine|Hircine]], the Storihbeg pursues the sun in an attempt to devour the orb and prevent the coming of the [[Lore:New Life Festival|New Life]]. [[Lore:Reachman Shamans|Reachfolk Shamans]] perform the ritual to summon the great Hrokkibeg to interpose his might to drive the beast off and shorten the night. They believe that those who hate the sunlight dance rites to hinder the return of Magnus, but their attempts are doomed to fail.{{Ref|name=NLFI|{{Cite Book|New Life Festival Interview|ns_base=GEN}}}}

===Skaal===
[[File:BM-place-Sun Stone.jpg|thumb|right|Sun Stone]]
The six [[Lore:Standing Stones#All-Maker Stones|All-Maker Stones]] of [[Lore:Solstheim|Solstheim]] are viewed as representations of the six aspects of nature, the six great gifts of the [[Lore:All-Maker|All-Maker]]&mdash;flora, fauna, the sun, the earth, water, and wind. The stones are believed to be conduits by which the All-Maker's power flows into the world.{{ref|name=DBStorn|[[Skyrim:Storn Crag-Strider|Storn Crag-Strider]]'s dialogue in {{g|dragonborn|skyrim}}}}{{ref|name=BMKorst|[[BM:Korst Wind-Eye|Korst Wind-Eye]]'s dialogue in {{g|bloodmoon|morrowind}}}}{{ref|name=DBFrea|[[Skyrim:Frea|Frea]]'s dialogue {{g|dragonborn|skyrim}}}}
{{NewLine}}

